From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Henrik Klee <henrik@goat-code.com>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 16:57:14 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] wifi: brcmfmac: pcie: Read the BCM4350 OTP
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Apple boards select their NVRAM by board type plus the module, vendor
and version from the radio's OTP. brcmf_pcie_read_otp() doesn't know
the BCM4350, used in the iPad Air 2, so only the bare board type was
tried and the board's NVRAM had to be installed under a generic name.

On the BCM4350 the OTP sits behind ChipCommon like on the BCM4355 and
BCM4364. Its size comes from the ChipCommon capabilities: the 40 nm OTP
holds (CC_CAP_OTPSIZE + 1) Kbit, shadowed from offset 0x800. A capture
from an iPad Air 2 (ChipCommon rev 47, size encoding 5, 384 words) has
Apple's system-vendor record at 0x8c0, as on the other Apple chips, with
the complete module/vendor/version tuple and "s=C2". Read from 0x8c0 to
the end of the OTP.

Unlike the other chips in this function the BCM4350 is also used on
non-Apple boards, whose OTP has no Apple record. A failed parse there
isn't an error: keep the default firmware selection instead of failing
the probe. An OTP size of zero means there is no OTP.

This replaces a separate PCI driver that captured the OTP shadow so the
host could pick the NVRAM.

[Orchard: replaces mattonik/ipad-nixos b94d42f kernel/patches/0061-pcie-apple-t7000-bcm-probe.patch, 0062-pcie-apple-t7000-bcm-shadow-capture.patch, handed to Orchard by micro23 (ipadair2-omarchy 7ea2b2b); OTP layout from research/j81-bcm4350-otp-source-audit.md and research/2026-10-06-bcm4350-otp-parser-result.md]
Reported-by: Martin Puškáč <martin@icebear.sk>
Signed-off-by: Henrik Klee <henrik@goat-code.com>
Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 .../broadcom/brcm80211/brcmfmac/pcie.c        | 24 ++++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 23 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/drivers/net/wireless/broadcom/brcm80211/brcmfmac/pcie.c b/drivers/net/wireless/broadcom/brcm80211/brcmfmac/pcie.c
index 1f3e7021b..d7c6a402f 100644
--- a/drivers/net/wireless/broadcom/brcm80211/brcmfmac/pcie.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/broadcom/brcm80211/brcmfmac/pcie.c
@@ -2050,12 +2050,29 @@ static int brcmf_pcie_read_otp(struct brcmf_pciedev_info *devinfo)
 {
 	const struct pci_dev *pdev = devinfo->pdev;
 	struct brcmf_bus *bus = dev_get_drvdata(&pdev->dev);
-	u32 coreid, base, words, idx, sromctl;
+	u32 coreid, base, words, idx, sromctl, otpsize;
+	bool optional = false;
 	u16 *otp;
 	struct brcmf_core *core;
 	int ret;
 
 	switch (devinfo->ci->chip) {
+	case BRCM_CC_4350_CHIP_ID:
+		/*
+		 * The 40 nm OTP holds (CC_CAP_OTPSIZE + 1) Kbit and is
+		 * shadowed in ChipCommon from 0x800; Apple's records start
+		 * at 0x8c0. The BCM4350 is used on non-Apple boards too,
+		 * whose OTP has no Apple record, so don't fail without one.
+		 */
+		otpsize = (devinfo->ci->cc_caps & CC_CAP_OTPSIZE) >>
+			  CC_CAP_OTPSIZE_SHIFT;
+		if (!otpsize)
+			return 0;
+		coreid = BCMA_CORE_CHIPCOMMON;
+		base = 0x8c0;
+		words = (otpsize + 1) * 1024 / 16 - (0x8c0 - 0x800) / 2;
+		optional = true;
+		break;
 	case BRCM_CC_4355_CHIP_ID:
 		coreid = BCMA_CORE_CHIPCOMMON;
 		base = 0x8c0;
@@ -2128,6 +2145,11 @@ static int brcmf_pcie_read_otp(struct brcmf_pciedev_info *devinfo)
 	ret = brcmf_pcie_parse_otp(devinfo, (u8 *)otp, 2 * words);
 	kfree(otp);
 
+	if (ret && optional) {
+		brcmf_dbg(PCIE, "No Apple OTP record, using default firmware\n");
+		return 0;
+	}
+
 	return ret;
 }
 
